<h2>3. Confirmed Rule: The Delivery Paradox</h2>
<p>Low-delivery stocks continue to outperform high-delivery stocks for aggressive swing targets (+15% to +25%). This confirms our hypothesis that defensive, high-delivery large caps move too slowly to hit high swing targets before getting stopped out on trailing consolidation pullbacks.</p>

<table>
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th>Delivery Bucket</th>
            <th>Completed Trades</th>
            <th>Win Rate</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="font-weight:bold;">Low (&lt;25%)</td>
            <td>636</td>
            <td class="highlight-win">35.53%</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>Medium (25-50%)</td>
            <td>104</td>
            <td>40.38%</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="color:#C00000; font-weight:bold;">High (&gt;50%)</td>
            <td>41</td>
            <td class="highlight-fail">19.51%</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2>4. Golden Trade Selection Rules</h2>
<div class="note-box">
    <strong>1. Prioritize Sectors</strong>: Focus exclusively on VCP/Flag setups in <strong>Leading</strong> or <strong>Emerging</strong> categories. This yields a massive success rate (~65% - 90%).<br>
    <strong>2. Avoid Low-Quality Groups</strong>: Instantly screen out stocks in **Unscaled** or **Weak** sectors with low AMS scores (< 60) to eliminate 64%+ of stopped-out losses.<br>
    <strong>3. Calibrate Target Sizing</strong>: For large-cap, high-delivery stocks (>50%), secure profits early at Target 1 (+8% to +10%) or use a tighter trailing stop.
</div>

